---
// ABOUTME: Kryptos sculpture page: the story of K1-K4 with one-click decryption of K1 and K2.
// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Michel Oosterhof
// SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { buttonClass } from "../styles/form";

const K1 = "EMUFPHZLRFAXYUSDJKZLDKRNSHGNFIVJYQTQUXQBQVYUVLLTREVJYQTMKYRDMFD";
const K2 =
  "VFPJUDEEHZWETZYVGWHKKQETGFQJNCEGGWHKK?DQMCPFQZDQMMIAGPFXHQRLG" +
  "TIMVMZJANQLVKQEDAGDVFRPJUNGEUNAQZGZLECGYUXUEENJTBJLBQCRTBJDFHRR" +
  "YIZETKZEMVDUFKSJHKFWHKUWQLSZFTIHHDDDUVH?DWKBFUFPWNTDFIYCUQZERE" +
  "EVLDKFEZMOQQJLTTUGSYQPFEUNLAVIDXFLGGTEZ?FKZBSFDQVGOGIPUFXHHDRKF" +
  "FHQNTGPUAECNUVPDJMQCLQUMUNEDFQELZZVRRGKFFVOEEXBDMVPNFQXEZLGRE" +
  "DNQFMPNZGLFLPMRJQYALMGNUVPDXVKPDQUMEBEDMHDAFMJGZNUPLGEWJLLAETG";

const decryptUrl = (text: string, key: string): string =>
  `/quagmire/?text=${encodeURIComponent(text)}&variant=3&keyword=KRYPTOS&key=${key}&indicator=K&mode=decrypt`;

const linkClass =
  "text-orange-700 underline-offset-2 hover:underline dark:text-orange-400";
---

<Base
  title="Kryptos — decrypt K1 and K2 yourself"
  description="The CIA's Kryptos sculpture: decrypt sections K1 and K2 with one click using the Quagmire III cipher, and read how K3 works and why K4 is still unsolved."
>
  <h1 class="mb-4 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">The Kryptos sculpture</h1>
  <div
    class="flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-4 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400"
  >
    <p>
      Since 1990 a curved copper screen by the artist Jim Sanborn has stood in a
      courtyard at CIA headquarters in Langley, its surface cut with about 1,800
      letters. The text hides four messages, K1 through K4. Three have been
      solved; the fourth has resisted every attack for three decades and is one
      of the most famous unsolved ciphers in the world.
    </p>
    <p>
      K1 and K2 are Quagmire III ciphers — Vigenère variants over an alphabet
      mixed with the keyword KRYPTOS. Sanborn even carved the cipher table onto
      the sculpture's other half. Because every tool on this site runs in your
      browser, you can decrypt the real ciphertext yourself.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-10 mb-4 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">
    K1 — key PALIMPSEST
  </h2>
  <div
    class="flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-4 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400"
  >
    <p class="overflow-x-auto font-mono text-sm">{K1}</p>
    <p>
      Sixty-three letters that decrypt to a sentence of Sanborn's own, with the
      deliberate misspelling IQLUSION.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={decryptUrl(K1, "PALIMPSEST")} class={buttonClass}>
        Decrypt K1 in the Quagmire tool
      </a>
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-10 mb-4 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">
    K2 — key ABSCISSA
  </h2>
  <div
    class="flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-4 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400"
  >
    <p>
      The longest solved section: 369 letters pointing to something "buried out
      there", complete with compass coordinates. The carved text contains a
      famous flaw — Sanborn omitted a letter, so the decryption ends IDBYROWS
      where he intended X LAYER TWO. He confirmed the correction in 2006. The
      misspellings UNDERGRUUND and DESPARATLY elsewhere are deliberate.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={decryptUrl(K2, "ABSCISSA")} class={buttonClass}>
        Decrypt K2 in the Quagmire tool
      </a>
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-10 mb-4 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">K3 and K4</h2>
  <div
    class="flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-4 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400"
  >
    <p>
      K3 changes method entirely: it is a
      <a href="/transposition" class={linkClass}>transposition</a> — the letters of
      a passage from Howard Carter's diary on opening Tutankhamun's tomb ("Can you
      see anything?") are rearranged by a double rotation rather than substituted.
      The <a href="/analyze" class={linkClass}>cipher identifier</a> shows the telltale
      signature: English letter frequencies, unreadable text.
    </p>
    <p>
      K4 is 97 letters and still unsolved. Sanborn has released four plaintext
      cribs — BERLIN, CLOCK, EAST NORTHEAST, and BERLIN CLOCK — and the known
      letter positions rule out every simple system on this site, which is
      exactly why it endures. In 2025 Sanborn auctioned the solution, sealed,
      for the benefit of his estate; the cipher itself remains open to anyone
      with a pencil.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
